  <!-- ARM INERTIALS (Link1..Link7) — RealMan's own mass properties, 2026-08-30.

         SOURCE. `libalgo_get_link_dynamics_params_default` after
         `rm_algo_init_sys_data(RM_MODEL_RM_75_E, RM_MODEL_RM_ISF_E)`: a 7x10
         barycentric table [Ixx,Ixy,Ixz,Iyy,Iyz,Izz, m*cx,m*cy,m*cz, m] about
         each LINK ORIGIN in the vendor's frames. Reverse parallel-axis moves it
         to the COM; a signed-permutation rotation (+-90 deg about x, alternating,
         identity at link 7) carries it into these frames. The frame map is taken
         from FK at q=0, not assumed: origins coincide to 1.7 um on links 1-6
         and every joint axis agrees in the base frame. Link 7 is the exception —
         our frame sits 2.8 mm further out (the d7 NOTE below). No translation
         correction is applied there: the COM is a property of the body in its
         own mesh frame, and link7.STL is the vendor's. Regenerate or re-check
         with tools/kinematics/check_link_dynamics.py.

         CORROBORATION. RealMan publish RM75-6FB.urdf at
         github.com/RealManRobot/rm_models/tree/main/RM75. Its link_1..link_6
         inertials are the SAME NUMBERS as the SDK table — COMs identical
         component-by-component, eigenvalues about the COM within 0.31 % — so the
         table is CAD mass properties, not identification output. Two independent
         artefacts, one body. (The published file has a typo at link_3: it prints
         cy = -0.013980 where the SDK has -0.010398. The SDK value is the one that
         reproduces the published COM tensor under parallel-axis to 0.002 %; the
         published one leaves a 4.6 % residual. We use the SDK's.)

         WHAT THIS REPLACED, and why it had to go. The previous values came
         from `rm_75_description.csv`, sitting next to this file — a SolidWorks
         export of the RM75-B variant with NO MATERIALS ASSIGNED. Read its Mass
         column: 0.5936, 0.4329, 0.4313, 0.2896, 0.2394, 0.2188, 0.0649 and
         0.8411 for the base. Those are the STL volumes at 1000 kg/m3. Water.
         Its COMs are the old ones character for character, and its joint7 sits
         at 0.144 — the B variant, 17.2 mm short of our ISF arm (F15).

         Whoever built this file copied that table's COMs, substituted the 6FB
         masses, and scaled the tensors by the mass ratio: for Link1,
         3.3575e-3 / 1.2661e-3 = 2.652 = 1.574 / 0.59354, exactly. Two
         independent confirmations that the result is pure geometry: integrating
         our own STL shells at uniform density reproduces the old COMs to
         0.02-0.08 mm on links 2-6 and 0.01 mm on base_link; and RealMan's
         published RM75-B.csv carries the same numbers. They described the SHAPE
         of each link and nothing about where the metal is. Every one of the
         seven COMs moves toward the joint motors here (12.7-31.2 mm), which is
         what a steel rotor inside an aluminium shell does.

         Link1 was worse still: our own mesh integrates to (0, +0.0252,
         -0.0211) and the old inertial read (0, 0, -0.0211) — the 25.2 mm y
         component was simply ZEROED in transcription, and the CSV row still
         carries it. It was the one link whose old COM did not match its mesh,
         and now we know why.

         `rm_75_description.csv` is now HISTORY, not a source. Nothing reads it.
         Do not re-derive from it.

         LINK 7 IS THE ISF FLANGE: 0.248 kg, not 0.144. F15 pinned this variant
         against both controllers at 1 um (Arm_Tip z = 0.867700 vs 0.867699 /
         0.867698 measured), and links 1-6 of the table are byte-identical across
         every end-effector id — the flange choice only ever moves link 7. The
         published RM75-6FB.urdf is internally inconsistent here: it carries the
         ISF joint_7 offset (0.1612, i.e. 17.2 mm past the B variant) but the B
         variant's 0.144 kg flange inertia. We take the SDK's ISF row.

         STILL UNSOURCED, deliberately left alone:
           base_link  — BOTH URDFs carry the water-density tensor (identical to
                        RM75-B.csv's) while claiming 1.862 kg, so neither vendor
                        artefact has a real value. It is a fixed joint, so it
                        contributes nothing to arm joint torque.
         RESOLVED 2026-09-06 — CameraHolderLink / ConnectorLink. These carried 0.5 kg
         round numbers, alix's own hardware, never weighed. ConnectorLink's implied
         density was 17200 kg/m^3, denser than lead.

         VALUES AND THEIR BASIS. Mass = own collision-mesh volume x assumed material
         density; inertia recomputed from the same mesh at that mass, so the two are
         mutually consistent (they were not before).
           ConnectorLink       0.0785 kg  29.070 cm^3 @ 2700 (6061 aluminium)
           CameraHolderLink R  0.0698 kg  112.121 cm^3
           CameraHolderLink L  0.0461 kg  74.086 cm^3
         These are ESTIMATES. Over-stating tool mass over-states required joint
         torque, the safe direction for a robot model.

         WHAT THE MEASUREMENT SAYS, AND WHY IT DOES NOT SETTLE THESE. The arms' own
         load identification reads 0.567 kg left and 0.711 kg right (payload_audit;
         alix_emulator _PAYLOAD_CONSENSUS). Both arms carry the same hand, the same
         rubber and the same ConnectorLink, so those cancel in the DIFFERENCE, leaving
         one clean constraint that uses no description value at all:

             R_CameraHolderLink - L_CameraHolderLink = 0.144 - 0.072 = 0.0720 kg
             (0.072 kg being Intel's own published D435 mass, from their
              realsense2_description, not ours)

         The values above give a difference of 0.0237 kg, so THEY DO NOT SATISFY IT.
         Satisfying it at equal density would need ~0.212 / ~0.140 kg, which in turn
         contradicts Inspire's published 540 g bare-hand figure once the left arm's
         total is accounted for. The absolute totals and the difference cannot both be
         honoured, so the discrepancy is left visible here rather than papered over.
         UNRESOLVED, and it is a real open item.

         ON THE HAND DESCRIPTION, and its scope. RH56DFX_{R,L}_8_19 is Inspire's own
         SolidWorks export; its PER-LINK masses and inertias are not authoritative
         (Newton, 2026-09-06) — several links carry the unassigned-material signature,
         mass = volume x ~1000 kg/m^3. Its TOTAL is now anchored to Inspire's published
         540 g and is trustworthy. So: use the hand's total, never an individual hand
         link, as evidence for anything here. This caution applies to the HAND FILES
         ONLY — the arm inertials above are the vendor SDK's and gated by
         tools/kinematics/check_link_dynamics.py, and the bracket values in this block are derived
         from their own geometry, not from the hand.

         WHAT MUST NOT BE CLAIMED. The measured payload totals cannot be reconciled
         with the description by adjusting the brackets: solving for them requires a
         NEGATIVE connector mass on every framing tried (-0.130 kg trusting the CAD
         hand masses, -0.138 kg assuming the hands are equal mirror parts). The
         system is over-determined. What the measurement does constrain is the hand
         (validated, 0.35 %) and the camera assembly; it is silent on the connector
         and the left holder, and this comment must not pretend otherwise.
         The measured CENTROIDS are deliberately NOT used: reconciling them requires
         a negative hand mass, and load identification estimates mass far more
         reliably than centre of mass. ik/glove frames are kinematic only and carry
         no inertial — they are not evidence about mass and were not used.

         Full working: Resource/reports/2026-09-05_arm_emulation_from_telemetry/PROPOSAL_tool_masses.md. -->
